# Git LFS tracking for new binary filings going forward.
#
# IMPORTANT -- read before running `git add -A` / `git add .` in this repo:
# because this file now exists, git will show every PDF/DOCX/DOC already
# committed to history as "modified" in `git status`, even though nothing
# about their content has changed. This is expected, well-documented Git LFS
# behavior when LFS tracking is introduced into a repo with pre-existing
# binaries -- git wants to re-filter them into LFS pointers the next time
# they're staged. DO NOT bulk-stage those files unless you specifically
# intend to migrate the entire existing document history into LFS (a much
# bigger, disruptive change that was deliberately NOT done here). Stage
# individual files by exact path instead.
#
# Also note: as of the session that added this, GitHub's separate LFS
# object-storage API was not reachable through this environment's git proxy
# (only the standard git push/pull protocol was) -- pushing a commit that
# actually contains an LFS-tracked file failed here. This declaration is
# still worth keeping for environments where LFS access works normally
# (a real developer machine, a differently-configured session), but verify
# `git push` actually succeeds for any commit that adds a new PDF/DOCX
# before relying on it.
*.pdf filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.docx filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.doc filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
